  "justification": "Operational Excellence: The proposed design implements automated Multi-AZ failover, continuous point-in-time recovery with 7-day retention, Auto Scaling to match demand, and CloudFront caching to reduce origin load by 50 percent. Monitoring is implicit in Multi-AZ and ASG infrastructure. Security: EC2 instances run in private subnets behind NAT and ALB; no instance is internet-facing. S3 photos are stored in standard buckets; CloudFront serves as the public distribution layer. Reliability: N2 mandates ≥2 instances across ≥2 AZs; the proposed ASG (min 2, max 10, across 2 AZs) maintains at least 1 instance per AZ at all times. Multi-AZ RDS provides automatic failover. N3 specifies RPO ≤5 minutes and RTO ≤1 hour: continuous replication in Multi-AZ mode achieves RPO near-zero, and automated failover achieves RTO well under 1 hour. Performance Efficiency: t3.small instances (80 req/sec each at 70% CPU) run at a minimum of 4 instances (baseline 320 req/sec), scaling to 10 (800 req/sec max), exceeding the 750 req/sec origin peak required by N1. At 60 peak hours per month, instances scale up; at average load (150 req/sec origin), 2 instances suffice. db.t3.medium provides sufficient throughput to handle database load. CloudFront serves 50% of requests from cache, reducing origin load to the stated 750 req/sec. Cost Optimization: The proposed design costs $610.19 per month, remaining within the N5 budget of $750, leaving $139.81 margin. Upgrading from t3.micro (40 req/sec) to t3.small (80 req/sec) doubles per-instance capacity and improves cost efficiency; a minimum of 4 instances at t3.small is more economical than scaling 19 t3.micros. The database upgrade to db.t3.medium (necessary to handle origin load) is offset by avoiding costly over-scaling of the compute tier. Read replicas are omitted because they increase cost without improving RTO (failover is automatic) and are not required by any stated requirement.",
