  "justification": "The original Stage 3 architecture cannot meet peak demand: two t3.micro instances (40 req/s each = 80 req/s total) fall far short of the 750 req/s origin requirement (N1). The proposed architecture scales to t3.medium instances (160 req/s each at 70% CPU). To sustain 750 req/s, we need ⌈750/160⌉ = 5 instances at peak. The design maintains a minimum of 2 instances across 2 AZs (N2), scales to 5 during the 60 monthly peak hours, and maintains 3 instances baseline, averaging 3.4 instances monthly while controlling cost and meeting all load requirements. RDS db.t3.medium Multi-AZ enables automatic failover (N2) with 7-day backup retention, achieving the 5-minute RPO and 1-hour RTO (N3) via continuous Multi-AZ replication and automated point-in-time recovery. Single NAT Gateway reduces cost; application connection pooling tolerates momentary outages. CloudFront, ALB, and S3 remain unchanged. Total cost: $747.54, within the $750 budget (N5). The design addresses all five Well-Architected Framework pillars: **Operational Excellence** through automated Auto Scaling policies that respond to demand changes and Multi-AZ RDS failover automation requiring minimal manual intervention; **Security** via private subnets isolating compute from internet exposure, ALB in public subnets as the sole entry point, and VPC security groups restricting traffic flow; **Reliability** through redundant instances across 2 Availability Zones ensuring service continuity, Multi-AZ RDS with synchronous replication and automatic failover, CloudFront distribution caching content, and Auto Scaling replacing unhealthy instances; **Performance Efficiency** by right-sizing t3.medium instances to the minimum class meeting peak load requirements (800 req/s capacity) and using Auto Scaling to dynamically adjust capacity from baseline to peak, avoiding over-provisioning; **Cost Optimization** by implementing baseline/peak scaling to pay for capacity only when needed, using single NAT Gateway rather than redundant gateways, and selecting t3-generation burstable instances that reduce idle-hour overhead compared to fixed-capacity instances.",
  "comparison": "The original architecture ($284.82/month) runs t3.micro instances that can sustain only 80 req/s across 2 instances, making it incapable of handling the 750 req/s peak load from the origin (N1 failure). It also lacks guaranteed automatic database failover and point-in-time recovery, risking data loss and extended downtime (N2 and N3 failures). The proposed architecture ($747.54/month, +$462.72) upgrades to t3.medium instances and scales the ASG to a minimum of 2 and maximum of 5, enabling horizontal scaling from baseline to peak demand. The maximum capacity of 5 instances × 160 req/s = 800 req/s exceeds the 750 req/s origin requirement, meeting N1. RDS db.t3.medium Multi-AZ provides automatic failover and 7-day point-in-time recovery, satisfying N2 and N3. The cost increase is necessary to achieve scalability, reliability, and performance; the original architecture's insufficient capacity would result in dropped requests and poor user experience during peak hours. The proposed design stays within the $750 budget (N5) and maintains the EC2 Auto Scaling web tier (N6) while fundamentally resolving the scalability and reliability gaps of the original."
